  "authored_scope": "Random mode samples odd grid sizes 9..29 wide and 7..29 tall. Default samples are about 60% square and 40% strictly non-square. The task fixtures are square 15..19, so other sizes and non-square grids are synthetic extensions. The line and marker are always placed in the middle column and row, so reflections never clip. Random shapes vary in: tapered depth profile with jitter, vertical extent (about 80% off-centre relative to the marker row), layer or band colouring with 2..6 colours, border thickness, an optional spine column, 0-2 holes, and an optional fringed end row. Explicit mode (size/width/height + top + rows) reconstructs the fixtures. Not supported: off-centre line or marker placement (which would require clipping), lines of other colours, shapes not touching the line, or shapes extending to the right side of the line.",
  "authored_constraints": [
    "Width and height are odd. The line is at column (W-1)//2 and the marker row is (H-1)//2.",
    "Shape cells lie in rows 1..H-2 and columns 1..cx-1. Random shapes use columns >= 2. Cell (cy,1) is always empty, so the shape never touches the edge marker.",
    "Every shape row has a cell at column cx-1 touching the line, and the shape spans the marker row.",
    "The shape is a single 4-connected component.",
    "The shape uses colours from {0,1,2,3,5,6,9} only and has at least 2 colours in random mode.",
    "The shape differs from its reflection about the marker row in at least 3 cells, which separates the 180-degree rotation from a horizontal mirror.",
    "The right half of the input is empty except for the edge marker, so the reflection paints only background cells.",
    "Most random shapes have a vertical extent that is not centred on cy, so the marker, rather than the shape's bounding-box centre, determines the rotation centre."
  ],
  "semantic_review": {
    "status": "reviewed",
    "checks": [
      "1132 fresh input-only point-rotation checks, including every supported random height/width combination; all original fixtures exact",
